<section class="section road-law" id="road-law" aria-labelledby="road-law-title">
  <div class="road-law__intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Know the rules we share</p>
    <h2 id="road-law-title">These are laws—not suggestions.</h2>
    <p>In Columbia, the Borough’s U-turn restriction and Pennsylvania’s pedestrian right-of-way rules both apply. Here is what the official text says and what it means in everyday traffic.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="road-law__grid">
    <article class="road-law__card" aria-labelledby="uturn-law-title">
      <p class="road-law__label">Columbia Borough Code § 207-46</p>
      <h3 id="uturn-law-title">U-turns on two-way streets are restricted.</h3>
      <blockquote cite="https://ecode360.com/7742902">“No U-turns shall be made on any two-way street in the Borough of Columbia unless they can be made back of the intersection…”</blockquote>
      <p>The Borough provision also refers to the times and places indicated by official signs. Its phrase “back of the intersection” is not defined in the section, so this page does not pretend that every U-turn has the same legal answer.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Obey every posted turn restriction.</li>
        <li>Do not make a U-turn that cannot be completed safely or that interferes with traffic.</li>
        <li>State law also bars turning around on a curve or near a hill crest when an approaching driver cannot see the vehicle within 500 feet.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="road-law__sources" aria-label="Official U-turn law sources">
        <a href="https://ecode360.com/7742902" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read Columbia Borough Code § 207-46 <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        <a href="https://www.legis.state.pa.us/WU01/LI/LI/CT/HTM/75/00.033.032.000..HTM" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read 75 Pa.C.S. § 3332 <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="road-law__card" aria-labelledby="crosswalk-law-title">
      <p class="road-law__label">Pennsylvania Vehicle Code § 3542</p>
      <h3 id="crosswalk-law-title">“Yield” gives the crossing pedestrian the right-of-way.</h3>
      <blockquote cite="https://www.legis.state.pa.us/WU01/LI/LI/CT/HTM/75/00.035.042.000..HTM">“The driver of a vehicle shall yield the right-of-way to a pedestrian crossing the roadway within any marked crosswalk…”</blockquote>
      <p><strong>When no traffic signal is present or operating,</strong> a driver must yield to a pedestrian crossing in a marked crosswalk or in an unmarked crosswalk at an intersection. Slow or stop as needed to let that person cross safely.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A pedestrian may not suddenly enter the path of a dangerously close vehicle.</li>
        <li>A driver may not pass a vehicle stopped at a crosswalk to let a pedestrian cross.</li>
        <li>At a working signal, drivers and pedestrians follow the signal; turning drivers must still yield to pedestrians lawfully in an adjacent crosswalk.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="road-law__meaning"><strong>What about someone waiting at the curb?</strong> PennDOT explains that § 3542’s crossing duty begins when the pedestrian starts into the crosswalk. Someone only standing on the sidewalk has not yet begun crossing. That is not permission to crowd, intimidate, or endanger a person preparing to cross.</p>
      <div class="road-law__sources" aria-label="Official crosswalk law sources">
        <a href="https://www.legis.state.pa.us/WU01/LI/LI/CT/HTM/75/00.035.042.000..HTM" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read 75 Pa.C.S. § 3542 <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        <a href="https://www.legis.state.pa.us/WU01/LI/LI/CT/HTM/75/00.031.012.000..HTM" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read signal rules in § 3112 <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        <a href="https://www.pa.gov/agencies/penndot/traveling-in-pa/walking" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read PennDOT’s pedestrian guidance <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>

  <p class="road-law__note">Plain-language summary, not legal advice. The official text controls. Sources last checked <time datetime="2026-09-11">September 11, 2026</time>.</p>
</section>

<section class="section civic-editorial" id="contact-representatives" aria-labelledby="civic-editorial-title">
  <div class="civic-editorial__statement">
    <p class="eyebrow">A ColumbiaWalks editorial</p>
    <h2 id="civic-editorial-title">Call us out. We didn’t know—and ignorance is no longer an excuse.</h2>
    <p class="civic-editorial__lead">ColumbiaWalks should have made these rules easier to find and understand sooner. We did not. That failure does not change the law or the danger created when road users do not know what the rules require.</p>
    <p>Without clear education, safe street design, and consistent enforcement, laws risk being treated like suggestions. They were enacted as laws. Columbia’s residents deserve to know how Borough leaders will make those rules visible, understandable, and meaningful on our streets.</p>
    <p>Ask for specifics: public education, appropriate signs and street design, fair enforcement, a clear way to report recurring locations, and public measures of whether conditions improve.</p>
  </div>

  <aside class="civic-editorial__action" aria-labelledby="civic-action-title">
    <p class="civic-editorial__action-label">Make your voice heard</p>
    <h3 id="civic-action-title">Ask for an answer—and a plan.</h3>
    <p>Use this suggested message as a starting point. Edit it so it reflects your own experience and views.</p>

    <div class="civic-message" id="civic-suggested-message">
      <p><strong>Subject: Please make Columbia’s crosswalk and U-turn laws meaningful on our streets</strong></p>
      <p>Dear Columbia Borough Council and Mayor,</p>
      <p>I am asking Columbia Borough to make pedestrian right-of-way and U-turn rules visible and meaningful through public education, appropriate street design and signs, and consistent, fair enforcement.</p>
      <p>Please tell residents how the Borough is educating drivers about 75 Pa.C.S. § 3542 and Columbia Borough Code § 207-46; how recurring problem locations should be reported; what enforcement or engineering steps are planned; and how results will be measured and reported publicly.</p>
      <p>I support a safety-first approach that protects people walking while clearly explaining the responsibilities of every road user.</p>
      <p>Thank you,<br>[Your name]<br>[Your street or neighborhood—optional]</p>
    </div>

